    "result_text": "Done. The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` create pipeline needed to emit an audit line on successful create. Rather than growing an existing function or class, I added the behaviour as a new small, cohesive step:\n\n**New function** \u2014 `AuditOwnerCreated.java` (single public method, no surrounding class complexity):\n```java\nprivate static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");\n\npublic void service(@Val Owner owner) {\n    AUDIT.info(\"owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n            owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n}\n```\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 inserted an `audit` step between `save` and `respond` in `owners.POST.yml`, so it runs after `SaveOwner` (id assigned) and reads the already-set `customerCode` and business-day-adjusted `registrationDate` via `@Val`.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **No existing function was modified** \u2014 the only Java change is a brand-new class with `WMC_other = 0` and `CC = 1`, so it carries no surrounding complexity.\n- The YAML got a 4-line step addition; the two `save`/`respond` neighbours were untouched apart from re-pointing `next:`.\n- Auditing stays a separate concern from responding, matching the project's \"one small function class per step\" convention.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreLogsAuditOnCreate` passes \u2014 the captured `AUDIT` line (`owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-01`) contains the id, customerCode, and registrationDate as asserted. Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
